WS73 Wi-Fi interface audit
=========================

From the kernel worktree:

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/wifi/check-abi.py

Requires the sibling ws73_SDK checkout, Clang and llvm-objcopy. Three SDK
headers must byte-match commit d5e68a58fe38c2b04a16769c69de1e8a80e86d74.
The tool compiles declarations and sentinel initializers sequentially for
x86-64, i386, arm64, RISC-V32 and big-endian PowerPC64. It extracts object
sections and checks exact bytes and sizes, plus ELF class/endianness/machine
identity and relocatable-object type. It does not execute the objects,
build/load a driver, open USB, or change the allocated hardware's state.

Records live in the private sibling .dev/ws73-upstream/records directory.
They include SDK excerpts, objects, tool/source hashes and expected/observed
bytes. Do not publish those SDK excerpts or treat the sentinel initializers
as device commands. The repository contains only the independent probe tool.
The first local attempt failed in ccache before compilation; disabling the
cache resolved that environmental failure. All attempts remain recorded.

Passing means these pinned declarations have the expected layout under the
recorded Clang targets. It does not establish the compiler/options used to
build SDK110 firmware, a device producer's layout, valid FRW message values,
real USB transfers, STA support or big-endian driver qualification. The test
deliberately shows why directly copying native pointer structs or bitfields
is unsuitable for a portable byte-oriented codec.

See Documentation/driver-api/ws73-wifi-interface.rst for the source call
chains, configuration/data length differences and remaining implementation
dependencies. No Wi-Fi hardware capability is enabled by this tool.
